WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.300
A newborn rat muscle fiber is wired to two or more motor neurons.

2
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.640
Nothing is wrong with the extra inputs.

3
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.600
Each one releases acetylcholine, and each one makes the fiber respond.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.720
In the adult, each fiber has one junction and one axon.

5
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.540
Two weeks later, the fiber listens to just one.

6
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.060
Where do the extra inputs go?

7
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.380
And who decides which one stays?

8
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:33.313
First, did the extra neurons die?

9
00:00:33.973 --> 00:00:35.273
That would explain the loss.

10
00:00:36.013 --> 00:00:41.193
But in rat soleus muscle, the number of motor neurons did not change significantly.

11
00:00:41.973 --> 00:00:46.753
Yet by the end of the second week, nearly every fiber had one input.

12
00:00:47.873 --> 00:00:53.033
Both facts are true only if each surviving neuron pulled back some of its branches.

13
00:00:54.093 --> 00:00:57.693
Losing an input means losing a branch, not a cell.

14
00:00:58.483 --> 00:01:02.243
Now watch one junction over days, in mouse pups.

15
00:01:03.123 --> 00:01:07.863
Each axon glows in its own color, and the receptor sites are labeled too.

16
00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:11.803
Typically, one axon withdraws from the sites.

17
00:01:12.403 --> 00:01:14.723
The other extends into the sites it left.

18
00:01:15.643 --> 00:01:17.143
The movie looks like a takeover.

19
00:01:17.723 --> 00:01:23.323
And the winner could not be predicted from how much territory each axon held at the start.

20
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:25.637
So what decides the winner?

21
00:01:26.877 --> 00:01:32.757
Activity. In newborn rat muscle, blocking nerve impulses slows elimination.

22
00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:35.597
Stimulating the muscle speeds it up.

23
00:01:36.337 --> 00:01:39.897
But these tests changed activity everywhere at once.

24
00:01:40.457 --> 00:01:46.197
They could not tell whether the loser is silent, or only less active than its neighbor.

25
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.030
Here is the test, in living mice.

26
00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:55.870
A toxin, alpha-bungarotoxin, binds acetylcholine receptors and blocks them.

27
00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.350
Block one patch of a junction.

28
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.170
The receptors there are lost, and the nerve terminal withdraws from that patch.

29
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.870
Now block the whole junction.

30
00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:07.670
Nothing is eliminated.

31
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.500
Why? With a partial block, a silent patch sits next to active patches.

32
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.880
With a full block, every site is equally silent.

33
00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.700
Nothing is less active than its neighbor.

34
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:28.900
Active sites can destabilize inactive synapses nearby, over a short distance within

35
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.480
one junction. Keep this rule.

36
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.620
A synapse is lost when it is less active than its neighbor.
